### DevDesk implementation sequence

| Phase | Build | Result |
|---|---|---|
| **0** | Project foundation | Runnable Mac app + repo structure |
| **1** | Local task manager | You can manage your real Jira tasks manually |
| **2** | Time tracking | DevDesk becomes useful for actual daily work |
| **3** | Context switching | It solves the main problem: interruptions |
| **4** | Daily history | You can reconstruct what you worked on |
| **5** | Jira integration | Tasks/context arrive automatically |
| **6** | Git integration | DevDesk understands your development work |
| **7** | Work AI Assistant | AI understands what you're currently doing |
| **8** | Salesforce AI Assistant | AI helps investigate/review Salesforce work |
| **9** | Outlook integration | Jira notifications automatically affect DevDesk |
| **10** | Automation/polish | DevDesk becomes proactive |

## Phase 0 — Build the foundation

Don't start with Jira, Outlook, AI, authentication, etc.

Create the repository and scaffold:

**Tauri + React + TypeScript + SQLite**

Repository structure:

```text
devdesk/
├── AGENTS.md
├── README.md
├── docs/
│   ├── product.md
│   ├── solution-design.md
│   ├── architecture.md
│   └── decisions/
├── src/
│   ├── components/
│   ├── features/
│   ├── services/
│   └── types/
├── src-tauri/
└── tests/
```

First milestone:

> I can run `npm run tauri dev` and see an empty DevDesk floating window on my Mac.

Also implement immediately: always-on-top, remembered window position/size, reasonable minimum dimensions, menu-bar access, and show/hide behavior.

