# Phase 2 — Time engine

This is the first technically important subsystem.

Do **not** store:

```text
ABC-123 = 02:43:16
```

Store sessions:

```text
ABC-123

09:14 → 09:47
10:32 → 11:05
13:03 → 14:11
```

Data model:

```text
WorkSession

id
taskId
startedAt
endedAt
```

Then:

```text
Task time =
SUM(endedAt - startedAt)
```

Implement Start, Pause, Resume and Complete.

Crucially, enforce:

> **Only one WorkSession can be active globally.**

---

# Phase 3 — Context switching

This is where DevDesk starts solving your actual problem.

Suppose you're working on:

```text
ABC-123
00:43:21
```

Then somebody asks you to urgently investigate:

```text
XYZ-92
```

You click XYZ-92.

DevDesk automatically:

```text
Pause ABC-123
↓
close ABC-123 session
↓
Start XYZ-92
↓
create XYZ-92 session
```

No:

> stop → select → start → remember what I was doing.

Also introduce **Next Action**.

Before switching:

```text
ABC-123

Next:
Check why AccountService returns null
```

When you return three hours later, DevDesk tells you exactly where you stopped.

This is one of the highest-value features in the entire application.

---

# Phase 4 — Event history + Today view

Now introduce an event model.

For example:

```text
09:03 Started ABC-123
09:48 Paused ABC-123

09:48 Started XYZ-92
10:17 Paused XYZ-92

10:17 Resumed ABC-123
11:24 Paused ABC-123
```

Then build:

**Today**

```text
ABC-123     1h 52m
XYZ-92        29m
DEF-442       41m

Total       3h 02m
```

This becomes the foundation for both reporting **and AI reconstruction later**.

