## So our actual development roadmap

I'd group all of this into **six releases**:

| Release | Deliverable |
|---|---|
| **v0.1** | Mac window + local tasks |
| **v0.2** | Session timer + automatic task switching |
| **v0.3** | Next Action + notes + Today/history |
| **v0.4** | Jira + Git context |
| **v0.5** | AI Context Builder + Work Assistant |
| **v0.6** | Salesforce Developer AI |
| **v0.7** | Outlook + automation + reporting |

The important part is that **v0.2 should already be useful enough that you actually start using DevDesk during work**. From then on, we should develop based partly on problems you notice while using it rather than trying to predict every feature now.

And I would **not start coding yet with a giant Codex prompt saying "build DevDesk."** Our next step should be to break **v0.1 into individual GitHub-style development tickets**, each small enough to give Codex independently—for example `DD-001 Initialize Tauri application`, `DD-002 Configure floating window`, `DD-003 Create SQLite schema`, etc. That will make the codebase considerably easier to control as it grows.